#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120653} :activity/id #uuid "288fdc58-d951-55d7-87ce-92acf93dd2ce" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121028 :tutorial/content "In the same way that we can factor numbers or polynomials, we can factor a matrix by writing it as a product of two or more matrices. In fact, there is one factorization technique that works for *all* matrices, called **LU factorization** (or **LU decomposition**).\n\nLU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/ad/adca162da15cab5989e5726acb495fc12533231c2e38b0392d68388aeb18493b.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/93/93338df4d1ee1791dc5b1679dbddff34d94d05677c362b1a41dcb925ca83a1dc.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, we can write the matrix ![](images/83/835b8ad372998cbb35be4c1998d236fe96f1d1b03ee1ad7062bdbbb77e254bf6.svg) in LU factored form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou may be wondering how we arrived at this factorization. Shortly, we'll discuss an algorithm that explains how to compute $L$ and $U$ for any $2\\times2$ matrix $A.$ But for now, let's focus on recognizing LU factored forms." :tutorial/id #uuid "827a0fea-203f-5c8c-bb6a-4d0764f6de04" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3176 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cca0ee93-f5ae-5db9-98de-167dafd23a32" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72"} :step/id #uuid "9ff11c4f-9a18-5d14-b5c7-e64d00a7d65b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2"} :step/id #uuid "bb1d2bfc-7ab9-5e32-ba3e-2e7219f0bcc9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a"} :step/id #uuid "ccd98e02-0c2a-5392-995e-e13e8079f540" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120758}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121067 :tutorial/content "Let ![](images/ff/ff7b3f4bca1676cbe3fb78ae1c2a47d8fd0bc71dd98dee4c7b6fea4cc94ec7bd.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/3f/3f74fdde04340f6a4dbd86962f5a31f8af22b5fb8053c66fa3e8be41629b12a1.svg)\n\nTo find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** To get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n- we can only add a multiple of the $1$st row to the $2$nd row ($R_2:=R_2+k R_1$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." :tutorial/id #uuid "4f9a2842-c4f1-577c-827c-41e33f514c31" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3977 :tutorial/title "An Algorithm for Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e8257db3-4571-507c-aee8-af086a0be8da" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120654}}] :activity/title "LU Factorization of 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120001} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478487} :activity/id #uuid "02e797f8-1f67-50ff-98e9-d7a3cb69a62e" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478379 :question/id #uuid "17c26653-7b83-5324-906b-245647d63511"} :step/id #uuid "1c48dd59-271b-57ac-b2ac-f6dcf7b5845b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478478}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478478 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243948 :question/id #uuid "3e43d37a-2ce4-531c-9855-25d3d30a5758"} :step/id #uuid "58e0c1e2-70f8-57d5-86d1-9e1ecca5166a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478479}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478479 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243949 :question/id #uuid "bd4d8bf8-4a33-530d-bc80-05a910fd04f1"} :step/id #uuid "5eb03b7b-af1e-525c-805a-a7fc08f84329" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478495}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399037 :question/id #uuid "30a5731c-2a02-53f9-9c62-2c24fdfa7142"} :step/id #uuid "b49cc272-671e-59d4-b2ca-4c2b37016619" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478418 :question/id #uuid "7301bd89-49bc-52af-80e0-064f8b80e2ca"} :step/id #uuid "ee4afbcd-af83-5653-a77b-3dd934feb1aa"}] :activity/title "LU Factorization of 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477727}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048743 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119603 :question/id #uuid "0e45ac96-0eee-51a3-83e0-192582dcf57d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4765" :question/problem "Given ![](images/e3/e34daf05ba492435f014fedd8336dd4bb4c65ae9c1688947d7873b8fba3b3e22.svg) find its LU decomposition." :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0c76f10-16e5-5123-ada9-c46ffc7d80fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117254} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc9837c-7702-5746-bde7-5a45cd35ac22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117255} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1b278a-4688-5c9f-be88-8efc4ef7dcca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117256} {:answer/id #uuid "368bae87-266a-53a8-9012-a8bbe2a28dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117257} {:answer/id #uuid "67555b5d-1335-5b3d-b929-cd5b7d5dc573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117254} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0c8d38b-40e5-574f-8375-b3ab7592221a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117253}] :question/id #uuid "5b6bfb23-75e1-5b07-8a9e-de9285790a99" :question/math-academy-id "q-32529" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,{u}_{22}=-1,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82e33624-2f76-52f5-a0ac-d0e5456b3125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300612} {:answer/id #uuid "afeeae29-79a8-50e4-b07c-0adaeb315d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -16 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300839} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7c54ac-bd1f-58b8-9af9-dde01fa69a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300972} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f94f4e-5030-56b8-be1a-60bbcb85999a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301082} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ed2340-48a3-593b-b7eb-7fa4a5012121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300612} :answer-field/id #uuid "165c63bf-c24f-5bde-9084-b5df798dcb03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301299}] :question/id #uuid "363a3b99-7f36-55a2-ab26-35e7a3af8462" :question/math-academy-id "q-32549" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -16 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -16 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e37e6cc-fad6-5510-9620-2cbac7ce22f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241439} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba5c239-540d-5f86-ac6d-97caed8a3faf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242416} {:answer/id #uuid "8bab7e05-3097-50f9-97ae-0da4430bb726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242417} {:answer/id #uuid "8eefa27a-e9ae-567c-9c88-944a22b88b15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242437} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d916f4-e1c3-59c3-b962-73fcbf193360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242416} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7fbcc62-56cc-5358-9e7d-035227d1830e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243840}] :question/id #uuid "3e43d37a-2ce4-531c-9855-25d3d30a5758" :question/math-academy-id "q-106762" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 15 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 15 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-5,{u}_{22}=6,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-30.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ddb432-c159-51bd-87f7-46377395435b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241213} {:answer/id #uuid "797c91c1-9cd0-58dd-9630-d65ae8a82a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242251} {:answer/id #uuid "95236878-0d84-5730-9210-7d52e16f2f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242488} {:answer/id #uuid "abfe08e7-1a8e-53cd-a0cc-09d44eab6540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242683} {:answer/id #uuid "e313faa3-97f4-5d01-bd42-efbcd8a6d8df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242251} :answer-field/id #uuid "491415e0-0ac3-5791-b266-069f3eb88034" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243576}] :question/id #uuid "bd4d8bf8-4a33-530d-bc80-05a910fd04f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106763" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00fcd0e3-1b06-5b0d-8ec5-bdcb973e0121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241193} {:answer/id #uuid "2f21bf4f-8fb6-51cb-b46d-2704e33c0f5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241597} {:answer/id #uuid "6459af2f-a6b5-563c-b06a-268a7964e5fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242083} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a1a69e-9cf6-54d9-af78-f001be097a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242650} {:answer/id #uuid "e0db43ec-63a9-52d8-940d-22db7f0c0613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243178} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfca5d8f-a9c0-5b49-923e-17f332033246" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243804}] :question/id #uuid "4c2a663c-d7cd-55d2-8a80-b4c4edbb70bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-106765" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 14 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 14 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44acbd5f-b359-597c-af07-f4df8077e41b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300303} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbb74af-0781-5c0d-a3b0-d192d46a427a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300451} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c014cd-34e6-5174-8a6c-ca3b5fd6d05c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300941} {:answer/id #uuid "d732659e-73b0-5b52-a39b-ff82b321e875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301032} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd631f2-7bb7-575a-aaaa-d8c9a58a508e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301032} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b767a95-e4b2-5302-bbb4-fdf21380f70a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301667}] :question/id #uuid "883a308b-5dbc-5683-ad84-c19d4f942e32" :question/math-academy-id "q-32548" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,{u}_{22}=1,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d223293-daf7-5ae0-8b58-c8aedf7d5f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300364} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7b83a9-fc50-5523-9213-c5c55335f7c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300448} {:answer/id #uuid "6be9bb45-cb39-515d-9e76-50406807757d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300506} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9a021e-530d-5a1b-bbbc-f93201721114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300671} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0b30d6-e077-5ae5-9c6c-d0df1cb8c001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300765} :answer-field/id #uuid "107612e4-db9b-5cdc-9b16-c8ff3e069e6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301280}] :question/id #uuid "85e17ab0-6cd0-5554-bbc1-74b553196159" :question/math-academy-id "q-32598" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations 