/id #edb/ref 17592186301543}] :question/id #uuid "468d9e20-789c-5294-9d35-e95d404e4f0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32617" :question/problem "Given that \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{7}{3},{u}_{12}=9,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=21.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0152189d-69e9-550a-aa74-d823899ff99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375696} {:answer/id #uuid "7339e9e1-c70f-5d2c-8de4-9188d1f4ee9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375697} {:answer/id #uuid "e1168d16-8053-595d-9565-1791421a6583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375698} {:answer/id #uuid "0004ccd6-0cb7-5f4b-835c-3676eacd47a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :