5a349cd678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555683}] :question/id #uuid "42d466fd-6c03-513d-b916-3193ae5d935b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106766" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=7,{u}_{22}=-5,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-35.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119604 :question/id #uuid "e1d1f80c-70d1-566d-9cc0-94899b0fa05d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4767" :question/problem "Given \n![](images/d5/d5f838110aeb104064744379d4879fb237a2ad9ad7b4728f4216d9f1bd39cd8a.svg), find its LU decomposition." :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b318a529-cb5c-5b37-96db-63c317afc535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117278} {:answer/id #uuid "f652fed8-2914-5671-88b8-ca2f9b7b3fdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117279} {:answer/id #uuid "133d29b3-ba83-5073-9fb5-ae8a15891606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117280} {:answer/id #uuid "17eb303e-e00d-5d84-a4f8-d39cb49ec3da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117281} {:answer/id #uuid "3695b530-f752-5009-8f87-09a944b9b94a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117281} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d9216ff-8580-5238-a204-fb582eb42893" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/r