y above the main diagonal. \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$ on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59f68716-0281-55f6-adea-b682838ac2b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441330} {:answer/id #uuid "73eed6d2-2a7a-5586-96c3-b4b57d716ee8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441335} {:answer/id #uuid "85f1cd67-ccaa-5df2-b149-878d29a40002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441336} {:answer/id #uuid "95e30625-bcfc-5d55-98a0-5886f8f651fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441339} {:answer/id #uuid "a96911ae-7ad9-5ac1-b6d0-ca8ccdeddc8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441330} :answer-field/id #uuid "99b3cb96-646b-5a28-8673-dce2e304d7b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441350}] :question/id #uuid "5d565601-7981-50a3-bc57-df1008cb679e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106752" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIII. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f014fff-e379-5421-b2b4-49d5a54f23a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441326} {:answer/id #uuid "48377761-7467-51b7-97c9-f6247717ea78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441327} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd73843-fd8e-5e43-8c1d-d6377ae8623a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441331} {:answer/id #uuid "6a875e35-4a03-5b66-9640-19be64465b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441333} {:answer/id #uuid "bffe5c32-5854-51db-b8f4-05b3f68585e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441342} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a6784eb-df62-5231-a320-70204c921944" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441349}] :question/id #uuid "73ab53ad-67cd-53a7-b27f-c766bd840711" :question/math-academy-id "q-75115" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $B?$\n\nI. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIII. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289d8b11-5b33-5df3-83e4-bfbab449cfa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441324} {:answer/id #uuid "374fcecf-456e-59e1-810b-f5c42633df35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441325} {:answer/id #uuid "55cc3f2d-2c9d-518f-83cf-c0d0c9b25d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441329} {:answer/id #uuid "64d9eef5-56f0-5943-8389-0af8adc56d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441332} {:answer/id #uuid "d54070f0-d20a-5ea6-aa9b-5964baf98a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441345} :answer-field/id #uuid "407f9dd1-cf10-5a35-8a2f-095674048c4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441348}] :question/id #uuid "8cd9aa05-4a21-5975-bf09-f09975b43169" :question/math-academy-id "q-32611" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $B?$\n\nI. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIII. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains numbers different from $1.$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a8adb1-6585-5d88-b2a9-a4c66146a7ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555080} {:answer/id #uuid "ad69335a-fa27-5de3-98db-67ed27d046b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555417} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd4791a-30a0-5cef-b04a-83ca7b826af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555581} {:answer/id #uuid "f3907a7e-ba96-5506-8915-537e18e2116e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555598} {:answer/id #uuid "f77569c8-f2a6-5605-a6af-f9b44459f38c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555598} :answer-field/id #uuid "5605219e-b32b-50d6-ae8e-13893dac1527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555678}] :question/id #uuid "55b8ab85-3682-5003-bc82-d3e9f5248d03" :question/math-academy-id "q-106755" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1779 :topic/title "LU Factorization of 2x2 Matrices"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125361} :activity/id #uuid "e6e3df9f-205d-5b79-a182-6a881367dca2" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125768 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf x \\in V$ for some vector space $V,$ and that $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ are two bases of $V.$ \n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the **change-of-basis formula**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that\n\n- $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ respectively, and\n\n- $P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ whose columns are the coordinates of vectors of the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nAs an example, suppose we are given two bases $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and we already know that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n![](images/d9/d9388f46f84cc0c6b1d7e5cf43ee973085366ae4f44cfe74515c59bfc1994662.svg)\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and ![](images/f9/f99f37621c20057077eb7c6e23690a45159cfe1b665c6b88b4a604f8687728bf.svg) Then we can easily find $[\\,\\mathbf{v}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,v\\,]_{C} & =\\overset{{P}_{B\\to C}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{[v]_{B}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "827e9581-9fde-5941-ad91-2c364796e5b1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3217 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "910841a1-0516-546a-8aac-f188c965b763" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125362 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb"} :step/id #uuid "ad493885-054b-5d5e-a186-80a2c3801b8b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125363}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125363 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a"} :step/id #uuid "45243fec-9ed2-54d8-b6ef-0d2c0a1e7087" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125364}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125364 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610"} :step/id #uuid "9fa2d20c-055f-5364-a1f7-12e38362db0d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577"} :step/id #uuid "2c41659c-3fb0-58d9-904f-2296e677eb6c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125822 :tutorial/content "We've seen that to find the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to a basis $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to a basis $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn expanded form, the formula looks as follows:\n![](images/89/890f34433211786e2cb8aa71057de40bdf3b12678c2bbb5a79ef5e680555937f.svg)\n\nAlso, remember that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ and from $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ are the inverses of each other:\n\n$$\nP_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\n\t\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\n\tP_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} = P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cfffdf8e-9e9f-5304-8249-54a094cc7c11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4435 :tutorial/title "The Change-of-Basis Formula in Expanded Form"} :step/id #uuid "4c4259ae-ebd7-5d31-ae41-72156cefc066" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125362}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125708 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Now, suppose we would like to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ \n\n- We first need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{c}_1+x_2 \\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf{b}_1,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to solving the system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/58/58010a2a5206e3fc27e3d924de70cf32dcc92c3578bdb2821ace7278fbb223cf.svg)\n\n- Similarly, we need to find $y_1$ and $y_2$ such that \n\n$$\ny_1 \\mathbf{c}_1+y_2 \\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to solving the system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/68/68788aa3e388c5cba14662a947c1b7afaacb95f611c256d11a7a6d7614c352b9.svg)\n\nWe can solve both systems using Gaussian elimination. However, we can do that simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/4b/4b00a4cbd7831f6da6acd06a087d5453bd455a89eb81f6b3036b1b572bb064d1.svg)\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get a matrix of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {x}_{1} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 0 & 1 & {x}_{2} & {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the required coefficients will be stored in the columns on the right-hand side." :tutorial/id #uuid "d42ee304-2e1f-5cc2-b334-65d083ea7323" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13287 :tutorial/title "Using Gaussian Elimination to Find the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis"} :step/id #uuid "184b8c31-de1a-5e34-ad87-2f626c830182" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125498}}] :activity/title "Changing a Basis Using the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124710} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436253} :activity/id #uuid "3b5b7055-d6b7-5899-a741-daa60aef491a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386696 :question/id #uuid "6ed39867-0f3e-5f3d-ab65-536828c9ba67"} :step/id #uuid "4de2c56b-39c0-5e14-a7fb-e708db903e6f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436248 :question/id #uuid "0e4c9378-b054-5da6-b199-f46a138a46d3"} :step/id #uuid "4f40a8b0-4293-52f4-8c95-85c75998d506" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247379 :question/id #uuid "162a4f62-2177-5623-998d-5746dbddde07"} :step/id #uuid "77dfbfe9-ef7f-5713-be40-7bce1749b9a7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436254}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323807 :question/id #uuid "76994fe0-8052-50ba-9dfa-9fe567d9b172"} :step/id #uuid "a2ad54ea-289c-5268-9e48-3009985055d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436254 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436249 :question/id #uuid "8d0458bf-0ded-5bc5-82a5-015d07a4533d"} :step/id #uuid "f5e3b29d-0add-527d-a99b-25156b6317ea"}] :activity/title "Changing a Basis Using the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436235} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486512} :activity/id #uuid "123b57a8-59a0-5d9a-805f-fed4b8283678" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486397 :question/id #uuid "72bf6680-369b-5d8c-9e49-1546d6428898"} :step/id #uuid "50c856b2-5de1-5fc6-b8b6-216f5affc71d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351454 :question/id #uuid "49aa77db-aeb3-5225-bb69-221318751e9a"} :step/id #uuid "b02f895b-b4b9-567f-99da-8866008266c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486517}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125036 :question/id #uuid "618e1711-d591-5872-b815-9d14ad33b674"} :step/id #uuid "cea4b00d-cc11-568c-8bd2-36b2db4582ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486427 :question/id #uuid "ae7fdbe5-ac31-5cc5-8e79-33235643a822"} :step/id #uuid "d7f877e0-9517-58da-83be-55a89211a009" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486510}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486444 :question/id #uuid "cd3d1f8f-1ec9-5277-bd94-fc774bb919de"} :step/id #uuid "e7ff6d11-7ab8-5df7-ab96-cc2f9b5a933c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486482}}] :activity/title "Changing a Basis Using the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485749}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048769 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124308 :question/id #uuid "922b0f79-2c87-56f9-bf8a-18711a2a09d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4838" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. What is $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}?$ \n\n![](images/bd/bd2cf58c26a51ae147b3714556631e1df786ae6e56cde01f801d9d93f7c258f5.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C},$ we find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2b368b1-58ca-5101-9464-b2089e456ad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122409} {:answer/id #uuid "ca563749-40a1-5a7c-83c3-2254da59dbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122410} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5fb485-8c4d-585d-84a7-836230d0588f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -39 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122411} {:answer/id #uuid "2a53fe6d-d92d-5408-945a-3b207b02ee02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122412} {:answer/id #uuid "3c603e2e-4f14-5bd4-9c2c-c764aeb0bfe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21 \\\\ -34 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122413}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bbe99fb6-94c3-54c7-8693-049e133dc1e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122408}] :question/id #uuid "30439e77-5965-5f4a-8cd8-2e6c41e4ad1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36310" :question/problem "$P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ to the basis $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}, c_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x = 5b_{1} - b_{2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1716bdc6-24bd-510f-89d5-ab35a3bdb21f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440401} {:answer/id #uuid "182873b5-14af-5685-85b8-f194b83b4644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440403} {:answer/id #uuid "4741e426-3e99-5468-9275-81e55bc048d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440416} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e4e105-32ed-5a4b-aa6e-4d1ffb7d3512" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440444} {:answer/id #uuid "d88d77fd-b7e5-5e52-b6d5-70171e5b3e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440403} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f2266d9-ab0c-52c9-ae63-a9f7e1dedcc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122546}] :question/id #uuid "5c2ddf2c-2a05-5bc8-8534-d8dcfb7338b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-36484" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22ab4118-1c2c-578b-a192-94d125f02543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244618} {:answer/id #uuid "540a3ce4-d4fc-5be5-89c2-e99cde15da2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245055} {:answer/id #uuid "955f064a-0a32-5368-9e38-01f459cfa9fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245620} {:answer/id #uuid "9932c53c-31e1-507d-8ecb-4aff729c20df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245672} {:answer/id #uuid "c1967cd8-76a1-5739-b15f-6ab0c43023c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245055} :answer-field/id #uuid "4188a834-2e43-59e4-aeb3-a5e1b0cc5850" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246655}] :question/id #uuid "e92b7de6-a8a6-53cb-aa1a-a2e89d07afe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-113556" :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+4{b}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+4{b}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dae6502-4e7b-534d-9bd8-c53dee9b8adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244857} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5339b9-9470-5143-8dcd-ee79f06f692a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245127} {:answer/id #uuid "9927e17c-ce3b-5cba-88cd-822e83219f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245671} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe775fb-12a8-5495-b1f2-446026491776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246022} {:answer/id #uuid "c80894ab-60f5-5cf8-91f2-e04575b9c80a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245127} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9db8bff-4392-5e61-be0b-6086b5cea1cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246852}] :question/id #uuid "b0aa2b6d-5bc4-5e57-a220-576badff809b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113557" :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=5{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-{b}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=5{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f62d0f-822f-5dc7-97c9-7bf26ef3dbe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321157} {:answer/id #uuid "211d97a5-508e-5739-b4e0-19bdd50e3bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321395} {:answer/id #uuid "5126e114-bfbf-59a0-a5dd-983b59417e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321785} {:answer/id #uuid "84c4e861-48eb-548a-95ad-553b6de5abf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322196} {:answer/id #uuid "a8da0b30-6b44-5bcb-9e2f-99b6c4ad1e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321157} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f527b5a-91ac-5397-988a-c8c887f42b2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323198}] :question/id #uuid "41491635-98e5