gin{bmatrix}13 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef4749a-0f85-5a2d-a5e3-df28976db7b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436237} {:answer/id #uuid "75556eec-854e-532d-9072-0581f7715993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436239} {:answer/id #uuid "a95465bd-ca1b-539b-b564-dc5450e2c673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436241} {:answer/id #uuid "d0811b3a-bd66-51ab-9df6-b54f8983cb5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436242} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c4b9b8-c843-5d6e-98b0-ea1b8a725222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436237} :answer-field/id #uuid "54566980-7132-5fea-9868-d89904943d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436246}] :question/id #uuid "8d0458bf-0ded-5bc5-82a5-015d07a4533d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113615" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50b8fde9-b1bf-55f1-85e4-62be49bc498f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440421} {:answer/id #uuid "66289914-3007-51f5-9e3c-d8f37baa6eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440424} {:answer/id #uuid "9393ea56-5979-5ca9-9da8-6c232698d570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440431} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7464c3-9605-587f-8a52-c4730e9f6f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440432} {:answer/id #uuid "fc23b863-6877-5fc4-a239-7a47d1c36069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440424} :answer-field/id #uuid "821658a6-a7cf-5368-b555-fe934cdee688" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440457}] :question/id #uuid "4f73a710-afa9-5a82-9ac8-46435d104af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-113707" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10cf25e0-86f0-5251-8e30-bdb8e6de1aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440400} {:answer/id #uuid "17b92d00-0a4a-59f6-a147-1be3ac44c4da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440402} {:answer/id #uuid "27f96cc5-65da-5d59-b093-95e1e75c4935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440408} {:answer/id #uuid "507e0790-a1c9-534c-b2c6-1884ab0a08e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440419} {:answer/id #uuid "d8411715-1012-54e7-9e54-4b60a0e318b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440402} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb22dba9-7d4a-5544-a86b-ad8fa73d5975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440459}] :question/id #uuid "7ff42b5f-a341-579b-8b7f-9bd10f63610f" :question/math-academy-id "q-113694" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -13 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -13 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18dc48a1-5985-578f-8b5e-9016d734956d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440404} {:answer/id #uuid "1b76988c-1557-52a5-91a3-c4a65053602e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440405} {:answer/id #uuid "1bceb481-65f5-5e67-97b5-3ca99d15ac64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440406} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1b735c-b2d7-53c7-85cc-47c1e2305f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440409} {:answer/id #uuid "9d360efc-d5a1-5ea4-8321-aa2b837cea50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440404} :answer-field/id #uuid "4922efa6-bafc-57b9-a817-72a740df2719" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440455}] :question/id #uuid "92fb3400-1e9e-5f15-b0ad-6ea07e725536" :question/math-academy-id "q-82620" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 13 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 13 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 13 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1910 :topic/title "Changing a Basis Using the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093075} :activity/id #uuid "e1c96bbd-1fde-5a80-973f-5b7f6ca284d0" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093075 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093440 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrix ![](images/30/30880937eed54b00cb0ebe13bd2a46e2634e4193343b1db552b30836ed1e055b.svg) Given that $\\lambda=3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ how can we find an eigenvector that corresponds to this eigenvalue?\n\nRecall that a non-zero vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ with corresponding eigenvalue $\\lambda$ if \n\n$$\nA{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}={\\color{red}\\lambda}{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this equation, substituting ${\\color{red}{\\lambda = 3}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =3v \\\\ Av-3v & =0 \\\\ Av-3Iv & =0 \\\\ (A-3I)v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to $\\lambda = {\\color{red}3},$ we need to find a non-zero solution of the equation \n\n$$\n(A- {\\color{red}3} I){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $(A- {\\color{red}3} I){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the following homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A-{\\color{red}3}I$ is already in row echelon form, and it has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. Moreover, from the first equation, we get $x_1=6x_2,$ which implies that the general solution is\n\n![](images/fa/fa0625bc99de3a27874b5695e72c9fd2e41ab618e49228086df7efcf7e1bb04f.svg)\n\nWe only require one eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda={\\color{red}3}.$ Setting $x_2=1,$ we obtain the eigenvector\n\n![](images/bc/bcd87529387e7c400b7f5ccd896cfcbe3e42ab386a5b9d84b565f12752e2c4c3.svg) \n\n**Note:** If we set $x_2=2,$ we'll get a different eigenvector, namely\n![](images/83/83541fd42d19f0e3b17be1c36983d50d1382f36352168bce2d148126db7bf265.svg)\nNote that $\\mathbf w$ is also an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda=3.$ In fact, we can choose any real $x_2 \\neq 0$ to generate an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda = 3.$ \n\n**Watch out!** In the case of $x_2=0,$ we get the zero vector, but this is not an eigenvector. Remember that eigenvectors must be non-zero vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "c585e113-f22f-5b5f-bfef-b3b81cd21acc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3562 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a47c9656-c51a-58d2-a0dd-f88dacd21b14" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092233 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5"} :step/id #uuid "96b57363-6414-5170-aa47-40a20b85159c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9"} :step/id #uuid "4346b6e9-b1f3-5e72-a0bc-acca6bf07e34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093441 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A$ with an eigenvalue $\\lambda$, the **eigenspace** $V_\\lambda$ can be defined as the set of all possible eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda$ plus the vector $\\mathbf{0}{:}$\n![](images/78/78c2c84d57ef00f32675c6d56fa91af39e0680eadf05ec266b0bdab9a57a8388.svg)\n\nNote that the eigenspace consists of *all* (not only non-zero) solutions of the equation $(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, the eigenspace can also be viewed as the *null space* of the matrix $A-\\lambda I,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nV_{\\color{red}\\lambda} = \\textrm{Null}(A - {\\color{red}\\lambda} I).\n$$\n\nWe also have the following theorem:\n\n*Eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues are always linearly independent.*\n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/e3/e3c63b7efcb927079757e6f0e3e008a6f5038e11957524f51e1b1f45fdecd5f7.svg)\n\nhas eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-2$ and $\\lambda_2=-1,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are \n\n![](images/1c/1c5df8239dae6efdd15a27498a9535b855004e3570c8eff46534993b9ec1e75f.svg) \n\nHere, $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are linearly independent (they are not collinear).\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can be* (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent. For example, the vectors\n\n![](images/c3/c303fb73d6fc039bfa536d6f7de3103e774d913d2514c10c17d59fcf48d5bf00.svg)\n\nare collinear eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ above that correspond to $\\lambda_2=-1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "98b63ece-dfc2-522f-bf9d-260df06b75a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3563 :tutorial/title "Connection Between Eigenspaces and Null Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "b551a89c-7046-5a16-b776-1bf76d285e27" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7"} :step/id #uuid "80c21f2c-61fd-5d44-98f9-6a51e9812c73"}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092465} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444371} :activity/id #uuid "4fa28d9c-f288-517f-8180-0f9553b67b1e" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444365 :question/id #uuid "59c071a9-0532-5356-9a7b-f9737214176f"} :step/id #uuid "37c3a8ed-fc50-5c91-b1c0-b7f217640868" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444367 :question/id #uuid "d0ad3502-048f-57c9-928b-2eb350ba84cc"} :step/id #uuid "3f94626d-4241-5985-b60b-4e7b51ef0b27" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444368}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444366 :question/id #uuid "cc10999e-0e98-57c8-9318-5ca4b1f6c8ec"} :step/id #uuid "a5b46e54-d2f8-5763-b2eb-1a4ff3c797a3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444369}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092610 :question/id #uuid "f08571df-c501-5a24-a4f9-9bde44801775"} :step/id #uuid "cf52d8f0-fe30-5018-947f-1ec4c11f7b2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444364 :question/id #uuid "4056bc20-f830-546e-b11d-9973d25fccb0"} :step/id #uuid "db002dda-e464-571a-8c51-66fd4a8e84b8"}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444334}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092233 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092078 :question/id #uuid "3ea5361d-2508-5d54-acbf-46a57be601f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5483" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of ![](images/39/398028421c4e26ae608c211073d010cfd7860476cec0861cb986bcc7a9cc91af.svg)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n5x_1-4x_2=0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=\\dfrac{4}{5}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/10/105c85a9f0f6bc92ac8f062d52790babc3331db1283bf4380fad442418d870eb.svg)\n\nFinally, setting $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/82/82cec52e485c08116a7e07f4b9d5b01355e8bebdd4fb910535a001e5e979a099.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb1ce0e1-ca05-52c9-8017-a6b8ca9e014c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089826} {:answer/id #uuid "fccb10f4-d636-5fc4-86c7-6986b751b94b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089827} {:answer/id #uuid "048aa46e-2e5e-5ded-a46d-a56822f4b38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089828} {:answer/id #uuid "55d386b7-80c7-57d9-b069-c3459eb95336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089829} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6028fb-5b53-5273-ae14-8203c772a605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089830}] :answer-field/id #uuid "20302973-67b0-520f-8e7e-35cf4214c841" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089825}] :question/id #uuid "7e8f2a7d-84b2-50d4-875f-6192a0b0d943" :question/math-academy-id "q-18080" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -10 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ =-3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25dbdfef-762b-5e3a-9a87-f25a7d801f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444337} {:answer/id #uuid "3874f5d4-8dd6-5b8f-8a1b-a542d4134288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444339} {:answer/id #uuid "7b602e5a-cdcf-5c39-85aa-d6ee24446194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444347} {:answer/id #uuid "874aab85-54f1-59bd-809e-d0fbd59d4f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444348} {:answer/id #uuid "99948bc6-a92f-552f-8040-f4159eba3490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444339} :answer-field/id #uuid "68afe63f-78cb-575a-8735-eb0f15a48444" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089831}] :question/id #uuid "f08571df-c501-5a24-a4f9-9bde44801775" :question/math-academy-id "q-18082" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =0.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c86ed6-c2f0-5075-a993-3436c8ee5153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241258} {:answer/id #uuid "0c9de29b-dcb6-5ba6-8c33-a89b67d83c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241287} {:answer/id #uuid "12c4e7bf-b7fb-5184-aaa4-b3d3246f643e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241342} {:answer/id #uuid "1dfb5e6b-f8ad-54c2-86a1-60e3ef9fd3b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241436} {:answer/id #uuid "42103bb0-1087-5254-a454-560360e83120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241258} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bb463d5-52ac-5f96-bb49-b5010b16b054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243553}] :question/id #uuid "289e6098-fc55-51b9-beb5-8ca8c852ade4" :question/math-academy-id "q-107138" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244033 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b0e113d-fa08-595f-97c7-0b7458ab31b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242002} {:answer/id #uuid "993de940-9cd8-5a57-b6ca-3208d60d669e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242519} {:answer/id #uuid "9f045778-9e0f-5601-8931-4cfb8fa007da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242576} {:answer/id #uuid "bbfdaeb9-272c-5040-86b3-1a9f34a3f05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242827} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5cb74a-0e1d-502b-b9eb-909d771bebcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242827} :answer-field/id #uuid "443231a6-d579-5afb-b8a7-f6da43d9c287" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243567}] :question/id #uuid "00c1b149-2b80-5f68-aef6-a6515fbba71f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107140" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n12{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dd4ff79-2cad-5577-9875-d43d33b96145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346312} {:answer/id #uuid "71558a79-a339-554b-a780-18bbfb559ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346351} {:answer/id #uuid "9e820d6b-c973-50c0-8a58-b0115a074639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346733} {:answer/id #uuid "b71ae531-024b-57d8-8a6c-1de89592cab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346948} {:answer/id #uuid "b98b0a14-d68f-5b3f-83e5-1f2d84851416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346733} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c731f83-bf80-5ca1-a39a-fcc51046617d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347706}] :question/id #uuid "1ab0b5f3-c889-5093-b43d-5502efb2f927" :question/math-academy-id "q-80318" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =6.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7098124c-fdf4-5637-8adb-ae9b427fc4e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346338} {:answer/id #uuid "ac2e4f37-16ae-5074-a513-6e772a718d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346853} {:answer/id #uuid "d52fb7d7-ee73-507a-9567-d4fff255729d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347227} {:answer/id #uuid "e08b45e9-73ed-5504-913b-10cb52ecc264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347322} {:answer/id #uuid "e93fdad2-23fc-56be-b5fb-ffa02df3cd01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346853} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dc295e9-895b-517a-a335-294acbd67827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347851}] :question/id #uuid "127d67ea-4345-520f-adfc-12e0e0bd714c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80321" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7b5a780-a9e5-50cc-9005-b16227f04495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386245} {:answer/id #uuid "fbed1d49-76eb-5edb-b963-b6db5cccb4a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386246} {:answer/id #uuid "be7040c1-8942-5754-b32f-af9f21ad5f4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386247} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e8402e-8e93-56a7-b73d-fa831f43d89d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386248} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf8af6a-c26a-5543-95a8-998fa875785b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386248} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8004795-4ea2-52b9-a76e-527c374e5954" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386244}] :question/id #uuid "d5d9e801-eca8-5d4b-8b13-1bb066d0bb23" :question/math-academy-id "q-107273" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}22 & -40 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}22 & -40 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -40 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}25{x}_{1}-40{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -40 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -40 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n25{x}_{1}-40{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b19debf-b0b1-51c4-a904-35bba46042c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392012} {:answer/id #uuid "29bf7461-9df1-5ff6-80ee-dae6c8038ac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392013} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8417ba-313c-5e66-b5c5-fae846969caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392014} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2494fa-ce3c-5038-9cd0-2a0133e7f223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392015} {:answer/id #uuid "c64b2b45-1de2-54de-8a8c-c04141659ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392015} :answer-field/id #uuid "073f863f-8121-57f1-987c-a6421fc7b6d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392011}] :question/id #uuid "60baa1cc-5bec-5c5e-b7f7-4efe1f7a3de3" :question/math-academy-id "q-36789" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -17\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -17\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}18{x}_{1}-30{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-15{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -30 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -15 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -30 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n18{x}_{1}-30{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c57b3f88-81ed-58c4-9f8a-e3ad55ab73c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393998} {:answer/id #uuid "09c64718-21b5-5183-bfa9-c8d620351a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393999} {:answer/id #uuid "11ba0d00-edd1-5efe-9ef6-92af724df99d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394000} {:answer/id #uuid "0245bee1-56df-5fa7-a2d3-689c3734c164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394001} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba80e12-6c24-5b96-923c-f37fd22cbc55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394000} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dc5d1e1-a9ac-55f4-97d3-5950006030fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393997}] :question/id #uuid "507cd94a-0757-5af2-9b87-e9063e728920" :question/math-academy-id "q-80319" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =10.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b9caa56-a685-5eba-85e6-58b1c539fe80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444342} {:answer/id #uuid "710b4da6-db48-5b65-bf60-9ede0d11ab35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444344} {:answer/id #uuid "93fa9520-d3fe-5440-82f2-276adae5ccf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444349} {:answer/id #uuid "d01308f3-db2f-59f6-acb9-ee0ca8f9d908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444355} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c7c06c-4453-558e-af3a-c9ca908eced4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444355} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cd1c824-616d-5479-8fa0-71440386982d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444361}] :question/id #uuid "59c071a9-0532-5356-9a7b-f9737214176f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107272" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-23 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-23 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{bmatrix}-(-7)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-16{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-16{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=8,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4c91cc-3b07-5016-8654-1e54c7dfb2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444336} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae2c7ac-8cbb-5876-b813-d09d251d1e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444341} {:answer/id #uuid "6837cfd3-3bf6-53fd-886e-47d9319e3697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444343} {:answer/id #uuid "950b6719-ebc5-50b0-bf1a-61c50145ab89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444351} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ff0fb3-134a-5ed1-9b98-c963db713afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444336} :answer-field/id #uuid "441bef2c-d14d-5a74-8b1d-c02448720f85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444360}] :question/id #uuid "d0ad3502-048f-57c9-928b-2eb350ba84cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-107270" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =4.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1123d852-cba2-552b-8381-92943f50c468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446937} {:answer/id #uuid "22dd36d7-fc53-51a0-aab3-39d55a21db8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446939} {:answer/id #uuid "270f4122-201c-5e17-b091-b38dd1de9097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446941} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbf235c-2ab7-5f04-a833-678137504436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446943} {:answer/id #uuid "9695b750-78da-5c59-9e1c-40fb8948f0d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446948} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a06f774-759e-5d50-b56f-887b8f0a8b18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446956}] :question/id #uuid "111ba920-c11a-5d82-a4be-8a7616b01f46" :question/math-academy-id "q-80273" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0684715f-1934-531e-a1eb-44980925a4b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446936} {:answer/id #uuid "24127ac8-a8dc-5354-a501-9b79d9e9cc82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446940} {:answer/id #uuid "51a64db1-d17d-5f2e-8521-b7c023059e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446944} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e21edc-e126-5eb1-b060-bf1813c74e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446949} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e801b2-7526-5207-9e22-764415b22da1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446949} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae3ba2a-4875-59fc-9d7d-d6fd0283faf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446958}] :question/id #uuid "301cb6c3-bb47-52a5-b9af-9305c0ba1695" :question/math-academy-id "q-18081" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector  of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -6\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}10{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -10{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n10{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092234 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092079 :question/id #uuid "f23dcdbe-03e6-582f-8383-b07a1b2036c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5484" :question/problem "If ![](images/38/38d451c4fe8eff5b96fe2df730315c129fac48c05324c8603576a69898665e54.svg) is an eigenvector of ![](images/57/576b0cfcb7e902b8fcb9c98d25a2145f760618d9a56ed257f4828d6ab3b05064.svg)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=1$, then what is the value of $\\,\\dfrac{b}{a}\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1+5x_2=0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=-5x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/1e/1e196ae1de5d8582ec93c6f964187b9b2b85b54f3b23e4017e50a1545310348c.svg)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=-1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/67/67e700524b8c7acc8923fd97acd1bb77b391944b91c891e4ce9388b22575709b.svg)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{b}{a}=\\dfrac{-1}{5}=-\\dfrac{1}{5}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc02d67b-661f-5114-b99b-4fecf9959b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090323} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7636b6-14d7-5113-bee3-f346d2f16556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090324} {:answer/id #uuid "2e27ec4d-090a-570a-b5c5-10c16ab97587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090325} {:answer/id #uuid "3050e6c6-6028-5716-983d-b12f2b71e0f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090326} {:answer/id #uuid "4651dfd7-c459-57b1-a12c-fef1de09aa88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090324} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a5fcb6f-f37e-5e57-9ee3-490fed3c5994" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090322}] :question/id #uuid "1dbb2180-f084-5fd4-8f75-1faa7ff7aa82" :question/math-academy-id "q-36749" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-5}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "804ffddc-e56d-514f-b255-ea482d6e6297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091625} {:answer/id #uuid "af258573-1242-57c4-8990-b0609362a59a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091626} {:answer/id #uuid "0c45fb35-8f97-5164-bf56-e5ec3cc55635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091627} {:answer/id #uuid "3a380f12-933f-5d9a-8092-8b7c4434204c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091628} {:answer/id #uuid "704d61b4-ad73-5574-84d7-9229fa2fa912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091629}] :answer-field/id #uuid "55e7b601-56ee-5686-84cd-6dc9daf34318" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091624}] :question/id #uuid "16b68272-40bc-59ab-99a9-020ece0f405b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9380" :question/problem "If $v = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 3$,\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302346eb-6a0e-5ee5-92c8-9befa41ee40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241610} {:answer/id #uuid "4dde1d88-11a2-554d-bb89-c72bacd74e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241877} {:answer/id #uuid "53095f11-af1c-5bd1-9268-8ca7d1167662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241921} {:answer/id #uuid "5739ca1c-6f13-597a-b56a-dab2abcfb8c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241971} {:answer/id #uuid "968ad9c2-d797-568d-af94-47a7712e6fc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241921} :answer-field/id #uuid "787a8cdd-1206-59e2-aad9-a8ef3d2700fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243671}] :question/id #uuid "52bf0e48-d7b1-5505-8bf0-dcd53ea9fa66" :question/math-academy-id "q-107109" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 15 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{4}{-3}=-\\frac{4}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e2e056-bdc3-5fa4-a393-7c34a4777ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241244} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8eaad2-644e-5de1-8b21-7effd3c6925d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241313} {:answer/id #uuid "481fcfe4-3c47-53dc-8c1f-806723491f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241832} {:answer/id #uuid "7b90051e-5f6c-509e-a985-fc6754f1457f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242265} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fa7992-7dde-5610-b276-4f858472ac17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241313} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc56ee31-2bc8-5029-af18-96fe3c9c2a56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243796}] :question/id #uuid "e9180a9d-8bb8-5d9a-bdf7-f88ca64062d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-108039" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2979d6f9-9e8b-5c2a-bbd2-6cb6ea061202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285151} {:answer/id #uuid "5833a572-9e0e-5859-ad31-d96beeb25e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285597} {:answer/id #uuid "a34e68f3-1c22-52d8-ace0-254e11465787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286284} {:answer/id #uuid "c76196eb-778f-5f6b-9a91-4232d319cce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286613} {:answer/id #uuid "c7dfc3f8-ec72-5328-9ecb-22f2661d9d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286618} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c50ba8d-7a5d-59de-ba5a-875076c13339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287207}] :question/id #uuid "39a663bd-9526-5745-819e-7355498f25c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-18078" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=6,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{6}{-2}=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0132c337-691f-5bed-8eed-d4703830b285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354666} {:answer/id #uuid "0137b1d3-e5a9-583a-855d-e8af4bafc1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354667} {:answer/id #uuid "680cea23-e9b6-5464-b8d5-8935697c3f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355585} {:answer/id #uuid "819c61ba-225e-58b5-a728-f6b0e66f5c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355802} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e128c7-3e05-505d-9e35-f7182129fed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355585} :answer-field/id #uuid "41ce8692-d065-5387-926e-6baadf977e0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357024}] :question/id #uuid "1d5b8de5-8041-5436-aa39-27f99669da1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-9378" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =8.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-12,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-12}{-2}=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085635bd-dce1-5925-b4fe-de2210a5c78c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354738} {:answer/id #uuid "2482f9df-8528-5027-b294-ef43187efdb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354969} {:answer/id #uuid "537c203c-ffd2-5502-a11b-30b204ae3ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355406} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1fcb5d-0201-5258-aa3e-803953e23aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355746} {:answer/id #uuid "7c520c67-acfd-50ce-97ec-e3cbae30718e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355746} :answer-field/id #uuid "05c8c7c2-f9fb-500b-a17a-b3feb5a918f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356921}] :question/id #uuid "ca1d168a-973f-5cf3-be95-af0c79523ae0" :question/math-academy-id "q-9379" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0 &=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "152f16d1-7717-52d3-80d1-4e93f7e18085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426795} {:answer/id #uuid "7b33cfbb-3ab3-5640-a98a-1b640acd1fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426801} {:answer/id #uuid "93521080-c7bb-5183-8fdf-d1ef0f91ea9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426802} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8b7370-66aa-59a2-8b42-8c520344571e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426804} {:answer/id #uuid "f1063d93-3165-53f2-b0c5-7b6d7f584b7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426802} :answer-field/id #uuid "354dd761-ee66-534a-8529-b3848bd7316c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426809}] :question/id #uuid "a278ff29-60c3-57db-888f-d20c4575a799" :question/math-academy-id "q-36763" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =0.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7b31d6-cbf3-5cfa-90f0-9eb9c7ff1c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446938} {:answer/id #uuid "3455a04a-3d31-5874-9183-71fedec9fd3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446942} {:answer/id #uuid "708ac4ba-c186-57c1-8e54-0b82ac4b423d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446946} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7d641b-bdc6-59a8-8ec2-2c684ab7d2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446947} {:answer/id #uuid "c26f161b-d55c-5ad7-8a54-c13c5aa07aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446946} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd938821-c74e-5b26-90b9-683b5bbf18c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446959}] :question/id #uuid "79a5f235-c3e4-525b-8b8a-0996cfbfc3bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107107" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-1}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092080 :question/id #uuid "b59a8ad2-7233-54c2-9055-5e188e0b8503" :question/math-academy-id "e-5485" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda=1$ and $\\lambda=-4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n![](images/b1/b1b6bc95f7d29e0c64007e7c19a4690eb3def3e58d677d8a21604315bbb21c0a.svg)\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "We can get two linearly independent eigenvectors by simply finding eigenvectors corresponding to the different eigenvalues.\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/81/812c2f9dfcb3943cc13c47d2f1b85d9b1a67ce10c9e6a965a9bb915655d0316c.svg)\nSetting, for instance, $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n![](images/7d/7d3a9e02893b98a2739398d48138be4c930ea87b3e3577776cfdd7edf6faf4e7.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda =-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-(-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{1}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/ea/ea5ff5d281eb5376520b2763f8dd96a926d52b6485452dd60b741b48ad0d7def.svg)\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n![](images/db/db85af375c67eca1f1d197a284b99ef7baf9fdb543b376c27e02c3d837742b68.svg)\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n![](images/a8/a84725763b84c77f8ce4d4954fe125d5b30f735649769e1d87e700287f1c1ff6.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f5ef4b-8447-5124-897c-0df2e9b6f00c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091631} {:answer/id #uuid "17a0f642-860b-5c08-869e-71e0e160ad8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091632} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4fbaa7-db2f-569f-9429-a97ebe1ad19b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091633} {:answer/id #uuid "41494612-6321-53b4-a90c-8417ae2ed3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091634} {:answer/id #uuid "449c4a5b-a1f1-5c56-bb8b-8801cc996a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091635}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f86007be-8133-5df3-8513-89898b43ec12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091630}] :question/id #uuid "2e377f6d-ebb4-5013-851d-eb5bd50e401b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9382" :question/problem "Given that $λ = 1$ and $λ = 5$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b548ab1-0a66-5f12-ae34-9e2bbd56f860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355028} {:answer/id #uuid "421182cd-cf5b-5e84-830e-43c0a355a65d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355248} {:answer/id #uuid "86084e8b-650b-5404-92a6-94060cc4d395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355846} {:answer/id #uuid "c2dd9a1f-ad0c-5748-8ba4-af058212730b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356368} {:answer/id #uuid "d2fc725a-d778-5b62-bfd8-6c1c94b53d64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356368} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc06244-dd02-54ee-8685-e340fbffe006" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357141}] :question/id #uuid "2b43c2e2-c2eb-5c55-afdb-fa448402d1cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-9384" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 11\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 11\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 14\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-7{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 14{x}_{1}+14{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 14 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 11\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-14 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-14{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 14{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-14 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-14 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f893330-42b5-56f2-9220-00273e71b609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241750} {:answer/id #uuid "53dd0cbf-1108-5612-85c1-5408607627c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241931} {:answer/id #uuid "7007dddf-e54c-5993-b095-65efb51f09ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242178} {:answer/id #uuid "b389358f-191d-50dd-bbca-67f426593422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242746} {:answer/id #uuid "c7204dd1-3ec6-5487-b47e-cf21b00eaf28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242746} :answer-field/id #uuid "1736c56f-53d5-57a8-ab4b-915da45ce5e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243477}] :question/id #uuid "5874be66-1b30-5ade-b2d0-93cee1c2df7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-107305" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =3$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05115124-1a53-5cb6-8003-cea6ff11b78c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241223} {:answer/id #uuid "435f1850-d288-523b-a8aa-8916b569250c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241786} {:answer/id #uuid "5f1fcf80-617c-5563-90ed-1451b4a3cf47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242036} {:answer/id #uuid "dd949d5d-2970-50bd-9492-7fd82465e0d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243150} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fc166d-04ae-54f0-a64b-80c5d6d1ff2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241786} :answer-field/id #uuid "301ea14a-63b8-59ee-a296-94bb1854e12e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243529}] :question/id #uuid "7ec17ca8-f488-5d0d-a6c8-da1edb5aec33" :question/math-academy-id "q-107311" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =7$ and $\\lambda =29$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{bmatrix}-7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -12{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 18 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =29,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{bmatrix}-29\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18 & -6 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-18{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-18 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2985b397-f2ad-57bc-baa0-56103fcbe28d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241545} {:answer/id #uuid "3c885850-b720-5910-8481-3b8fc8554e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241719} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1cd1b1-11b8-5693-9e6a-6325ded9f659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241886} {:answer/id #uuid "d396b976-5f63-57a4-868f-2a52795a54ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243049} {:answer/id #uuid "f60345e9-7d23-527d-97be-25d556e71c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241886} :answer-field/id #uuid "c49997ef-2ad8-5ac6-8a41-2278890403d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243786}] :question/id #uuid "d85fab59-8e48-543a-b7a0-bd15b756ce1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-108008" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =6$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-5)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{9}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0e7973-2bf9-5f0c-8dd7-5255df51d9ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345447} {:answer/id #uuid "2084e431-7940-5e9b-9aad-cc72a2f7172c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345639} {:answer/id #uuid "bba6104b-7d29-5031-9415-d138f6b6a1e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346979} {:answer/id #uuid "eae52c7e-e8f9-542b-a3b0-ad12b106e8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347423} {:answer/id #uuid "f02646bf-8450-5c94-8029-3d36d8d998fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346979} :answer-field/id #uuid "7adbbbe8-ab6b-5099-bc13-486b0b5c1e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347823}] :question/id #uuid "4d98964f-3df0-5437-bc4a-3bb084da1d43" :question/math-academy-id "q-80324" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =-1$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e6aa20-e17f-549a-9175-cec3e7f0a8fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345495} {:answer/id #uuid "3a50d63e-118d-5aa0-b97e-dc43bdd5453f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345850} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3025be-d655-5ac6-b428-87f9a6ce7ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346019} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9149e1-d912-5747-b29a-bd1a312db77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346121} {:answer/id #uuid "96e47df5-5290-5999-8bc4-fef32aa25e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345850} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e321d9-f60b-544d-82eb-34fd680ca77b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347817}] :question/id #uuid "3ce7471b-9a77-56e2-b53e-fffca538d26a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80327" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6f085f-023d-5ff4-9956-1bfb1673463e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355065} {:answer/id #uuid "42f9008b-e6f3-53d0-a283-d1bfc2d73f01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355259} {:answer/id #uuid "659352cc-dc42-517c-84d6-9f15c0bf6549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355567} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1c03cc-63f0-50e0-ac22-e5e264bab4b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356066} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec77a9a-c96b-5494-ad73-f0d2f735a7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355567} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1ea6244-1edc-5915-a587-20156e462eef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357320}] :question/id #uuid "96f1b97e-7350-56fc-a13d-baf13d00782b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9383" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f44c7b5-9e57-58b2-aecf-1e41d4eda582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355229} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4c95f3-9b59-525f-bfdf-f63744200e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355230} {:answer/id #uuid "4aaec6d5-2255-5a61-bcea-7ab17d4b1d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355331} {:answer/id #uuid "e322b617-873b-5239-8581-bb1d53e80e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356651} {:answer/id #uuid "f0afc2da-f659-52dc-b243-cb6a4a0f18b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355230} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ff30be3-7e69-5d0b-b60e-3dc0ccdae2fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357113}] :question/id #uuid "0000fc83-77f5-5913-a1b9-b3ae53df5b9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-9385" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c3b6eb-d05a-563e-9054-1a09875cbcca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426793} {:answer/id #uuid "22e10d12-85c5-5f27-8869-ab11e85fc4ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426796} {:answer/id #uuid "5abf6ee9-af9e-57ac-8483-5a3e973653e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426799} {:answer/id #uuid "69549ed7-5e3c-59e7-9843-4f93cd72fb1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426800} {:answer/id #uuid "e893480f-5916-54cc-a5ac-b814cdf8c446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426805} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f81bcb6-7113-5591-b120-34386b2b4833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426810}] :question/id #uuid "b7043291-4c2d-5218-b2ec-199ed93eadcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107275" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =8$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-(-5)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=6,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e2a0fd5-5d0b-5043-bea5-3d7c9cdc5bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426794} {:answer/id #uuid "4508942a-d2b4-51d0-a2a4-1cfbb3d6e36f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426797} {:answer/id #uuid "48e3bd55-0f69-5377-b778-7910fd7ee930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426798} {:answer/id #uuid "9624a80a-ce12-59e9-9acd-2860974a182f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426803} {:answer/id #uuid "ee78a4db-270a-5a24-94aa-7fd979241cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426806} :answer-field/id #uuid "1754e42f-ef75-5680-942c-adf751ac0ab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426808}] :question/id #uuid "ba90f51b-352b-5787-ba63-3059791890ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-80326" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =11$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-11\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-9{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "779b489c-1d8c-512a-85e2-bac696e42a10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444346} {:answer/id #uuid "98d985bb-4407-56be-9d47-f44c267d7003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444352} {:answer/id #uuid "a66df4ae-0975-55c6-92f7-f9ce6669e3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444354} {:answer/id #uuid "daab94e3-da94-5101-b40c-c342fca2628f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444357} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1d543c-5759-509f-a1c1-8ec85578fbe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444352} :answer-field/id #uuid "90ab5756-be68-58c5-811b-5048edcd87d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444362}] :question/id #uuid "4056bc20-f830-546e-b11d-9973d25fccb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108000" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =4$ and $\\lambda =5$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "164cc9ad-ccaa-5a7a-9d33-549c418ab267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444335} {:answer/id #uuid "3059f466-9e01-5a9b-9888-c5df1cc4ff7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444338} {:answer/id #uuid "4523266c-9e94-547f-99de-8fc67d5a1d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444340} {:answer/id #uuid "75cef35f-df45-5177-af4c-541c71dc484a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444345} {:answer/id #uuid "9458a7bb-4f38-5941-b824-335ef19a7297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444335} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5476fb6-80da-5fb0-9202-81c168d50130" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444363}] :question/id #uuid "cc10999e-0e98-57c8-9318-5ca4b1f6c8ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-80323" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =-1$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e2d8f93-5ef1-55d1-b815-c1515a040b2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446945} {:answer/id #uuid "a651edc0-0c43-5e03-bb3e-0a76e26d7201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446950} {:answer/id #uuid "aa601251-b637-5aff-9c87-3c9e773948b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446951} {:answer/id #uuid "b67258fc-1571-58d7-b3b9-ff66e2487d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446952} {:answer/id #uuid "f27efeb8-a34b-557e-9386-96c0b3190fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446945} :answer-field/id #uuid "99f559f8-f0c3-5b01-8f1d-d740f6f545ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446957}] :question/id #uuid "3beff671-5436-54ac-a3ba-4902dc435008" :question/math-academy-id "q-107306" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =17$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 15 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =17,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{bmatrix}-17\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 3 \\\\ 20 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-15{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 991 :topic/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101674} :activity/id #uuid "e035a7b5-8ab1-5910-8886-36efa38ee822" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101958 :tutorial/content "Given two matrices, it is not always possible to multiply them together. To demonstrate why, consider the following two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 12 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNormally, to compute the product $AB,$ we multiply each row of $A$ by each column of $B.$ However, in this case, each row of $A$ has $\\color{blue}2$ entries, while each column of $B$ has $\\color{red}3$ entries. The number of entries do not match!\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 12 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we cannot compute the dot product, and therefore the product $AB$ is not defined." :tutorial/id #uuid "15951cb3-8d8f-580c-803c-d989e64ec1e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2990 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "07f07b20-6f36-5da7-9065-e34083c05d34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140"} :step/id #uuid "0cf8a8d6-df08-539f-89e4-03e4272a9103" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38167e2-3c1d-5c00-9691-ba8e6ac6fdce"} :step/id #uuid "0df3419d-02f7-5df1-ad69-d0b480e15bb2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0a4fa3d-fc1a-5e95-a632-f3a25c9bc4b1"} :step/id #uuid "f71e7a6b-ca82-55ed-a10e-c28bb5ea6729"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101985 :tutorial/content "We can only multiply two matrices if they are **conformable** for multiplication, which means that the number of columns in the first matrix must equal the number of rows in the second matrix. In other words, we can only multiply two matrices if the first matrix has dimensions $n$ and the second matrix has dimensions $n$\n\nIf the matrices $A$ and $B$ (in that order) are conformable for multiplication and have dimensions $n$ and $n$ respectively, then the product $AB$ will have the dimensions $m \\times k.$\n\nLet's illustrate these ideas with some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "0df41235-ef9f-50ab-b91b-78caa512f359" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3800 :tutorial/title "Conformability for Matrix Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "f1cc4bcd-da1d-5aaf-8820-cac2e4f8bed8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101675}}] :activity/title "Conformability for Matrix Multiplication" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101057}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048783 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100869 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100691 :question/id #uuid "da92ef6c-cb0b-5a4c-9a0d-0f0a9db19616" :question/math-academy-id "e-4403" :question/problem "Let\n![](images/53/5357fcb0ec150699b4d50cbd6fa9651c421ac3500660703396146362160328a2.png)\nand\n![](images/63/63d354773d145f05e73e808efe2a21164cb28d6ff19ae104d6bbde6510cf10e6.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The product $A\\times B$ is well defined\n\n- The product $B\\times A$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, and $B$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{2 \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{2}}}{A} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{2}} \\times 3}{B} = \n\t{\\color{green}\\text{well defined}}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $B$ and $2$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{2 \\times \\color{red}{3}}{B} \\underset{\\color{red}\\neq}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{red}2} \\times 2}{A} = {\\color{red}\\text{undefined}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, while statement II is false."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562922 :question-generator/id #uuid "159e4b46-4092-5b2e-aef9-0c728d2fb3d5" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186100869-draft.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eee15b20-7670-503f-971f-d56fd86f8528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448504} {:answer/id #uuid "8e36cc5c-ee04-5d14-93c0-5ec4fd6754bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448505} :answer-field/id #uuid "73a25775-ae2f-598a-be5f-116f1c5257d3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449292} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a98bec2-fd5b-577d-b1a2-de624a75c4de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448506} {:answer/id #uuid "8f344679-285a-5a78-87d1-893db3de166d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448506} :answer-field/id #uuid "72b90ee4-8516-5880-9f21-2235262ae0b8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449293} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69c941f3-afcf-5dca-a631-aac8dfb757b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448508} {:answer/id #uuid "384c1599-b211-55de-9dff-459dd6139d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448509} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa235668-87f9-5d4c-8f5d-dd9e00466bf2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449294}] :question/id #uuid "9258b6d8-3a2c-54d4-bc2f-8b9ea4ccd825" :question/math-academy-id "q-22063" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $A,B,$ and $C?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The product $AB$ is well defined\n- **II.** The product $BA$ is well defined\n- **III.** The product $AC$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ column vector\n\nThere are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $B$ and $3$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{B}_{3\\times 2}{\\times }_{\\ne }{A}_{3\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $C.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $C,$ the matrices $A$ and $C$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{C}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 1}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ac97073-f2e4-57fa-bc98-21619911b032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477307} {:answer/id #uuid "fed5ba5b-9655-57ac-bf85-e26d4ca80ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477308} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9a32b98-4ce8-5ab9-ba0f-52336fc8a891" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477623} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab354b54-184d-5e28-ab49-c725761e84d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477309} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7c4fb6-9856-5e08-8e95-baf50093e535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477310} :answer-field/id #uuid "579c28da-6262-519a-b610-6fec803beaf2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477624} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f8cc3b1-c6eb-5129-bcad-4e7d08d4a6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477311} {:answer/id #uuid "88d68881-4ce5-5cc2-8b8b-c3129b170191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477312} :answer-field/id #uuid "058434be-b274-586d-9c20-7a526c76c9a3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477625}] :question/id #uuid "59f14d04-9b7d-5b14-8363-a42027c3b11c" :question/math-academy-id "q-42374" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -0 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statement are true regarding the matrices $A,B,$ and $C?$\n\nI. The product $AB$ is well defined\nII. The product $BA$ is well defined\nIII. The product $AC$ is well defined\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 4}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $4$ columns in $B$ and $4$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ equals the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{B}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{✓}{A}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $C.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $C,$ the matrices $A$ and $C$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{C}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that all three statements are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb45ac63-db12-5103-a680-0aca60a50ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448368} {:answer/id #uuid "a12d2978-9d54-59d4-9961-6bdc86f3c49f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448368} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0935ce5-8cb9-5439-9df5-d68f6b26b1b4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449224} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fb2ec2a-0f20-512b-93a8-4b9f3c962c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448370} {:answer/id #uuid "022054e0-5021-5992-9a69-680cffbd01c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448370} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2774138-c2fc-5876-84c5-93da8a175e9f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449225} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb2b2b09-edcc-581e-920d-e47a8346fc6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448372} {:answer/id #uuid "c8393209-b177-529e-b28b-4beba3d7980d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448373} :answer-field/id #uuid "887210a3-979a-5046-a639-10af689a9ba1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449226}] :question/id #uuid "22b7c2e7-9621-5013-a411-a722a7ceea01" :question/math-academy-id "q-129437" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,S=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $P,R,$ and $S?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The product $RS$ is well defined\n- **II.** The product $RP$ is well defined\n- **III.** The product $SP$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $P$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, $R$ is a $1\\times 3$ row vector, and $S$ is a $1\\times 5$ row vector. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $R$ and $1$ row in $S.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $S,$ the matrices $R$ and $S$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{S}_{1\\times 5}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $R$ and $5$ rows in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $R$ and $P$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{P}_{5\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $5$ columns in $S$ and $5$ rows in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $S$ equals the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $S$ and $P$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{S}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{5}}}{\\times }_{✓}{P}_{\\boxed{\\text{5}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement III is true, while statements I and II are false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a93ef54-75a7-58b3-aefb-23905447fd8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450250} {:answer/id #uuid "4b995925-934b-5fd2-877f-aa3cf84a5166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450251} :answer-field/id #uuid "399ae7fe-01e2-5382-b1a9-4e47af718b72" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451079} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ff3d40-849a-5db8-9cf6-5fac0ac794ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450252} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef26044-c1a2-592a-854e-9764c570b332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450252} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8966769-d214-5dda-9b5c-f23fef8c54ed" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e376649-41c7-5d9c-81ec-516f8c48228b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450254} {:answer/id #uuid "e18e340e-63df-594f-ab82-c85ec74e7f75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450255} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7bc0c6-04f7-5fcb-9611-2c846635a049" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451081}] :question/id #uuid "7f63e1f3-652b-58b6-9207-73ea266277c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-22055" :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $X,Y,$ and $Z?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The product $XY$ is well defined\n\n- **II.** The product $YX$ is well defined\n\n- **III.** The product $YZ$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $X$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $Y$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $Z$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $X$ and $2$ rows in $Y.$ Since the number of columns in $X$ equals the number of rows in $Y,$ the matrices $X$ and $Y$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{X}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{Y}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $Y$ and $2$ rows in $X.$ Since the number of columns in $Y$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $X,$ the matrices $Y$ and $X$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{Y}_{2\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{X}_{2\\times 2}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $Y$ and $3$ rows in $Z.$ Since the number of columns in $Y$ equals the number of rows in $Z,$ the matrices $Y$ and $Z$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{Y}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{Z}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fbf7dde-3a5a-5e3a-98c4-330f2d78c1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450256} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fc9501-54e2-5baa-bf12-feec5fed6f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450257} :answer-field/id #uuid "e46d5a65-585d-5246-84b9-60b6c53ced38" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451082} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eac938a0-2f02-528b-aa1f-cd4c2533f774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450258} {:answer/id #uuid "586a9018-9f0f-556a-b600-1b488eba0901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450259} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fd1e1d7-90e4-552d-8b7f-5890e83db150" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451083} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03abe8a4-3038-556f-860c-4cb48b3a36e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450260} {:answer/id #uuid "036047f5-2b55-5f28-bb20-54fcffca67ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450260} :answer-field/id #uuid "826fe441-a593-565f-921b-b7f4ca80e395" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451084}] :question/id #uuid "12577a38-9f6c-5807-b07b-3944030b826c" :question/math-academy-id "q-22056" :question/problem "$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,S=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $R,S,$ and $T?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The product $SR$ is well defined\n- **II.** The product $RS$ is well defined\n- **III.** The product $ST$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $R$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, $S$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix, and $T$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThere are $4$ columns in $S$ and $4$ rows in $R.$ Since the number of columns in $S$ equals the number of rows in $R,$ the matrices $S$ and $R$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{S}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{✓}{R}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $R$ and $2$ rows in $S.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ equals the number of rows in $S,$ the matrices $R$ and $S$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{S}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 4}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $4$ columns in $S$ and $3$ rows in $T.$ Since the number of columns in $S$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $T,$ the matrices $S$ and $T$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{S}_{2\\times 4}{\\times }_{\\ne }{T}_{3\\times 2}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d3c92a8-562e-563a-8881-3b32d1e12722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450262} {:answer/id #uuid "2aeaf99a-a587-53dc-8e3f-8c29f11b0bdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450262} :answer-field/id #uuid "5be89768-63a4-500a-973e-77e87c95bbd0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890358c1-6d1d-54aa-a820-9cbdfd1122e7" :